## 3. Requirements — Compute, GPU, and the Infra That Actually Dominates

**Honest punchline up front: GPU is not your constraint. Data plumbing and governance are.** Anyone who answers this by sizing an A100 cluster has misunderstood the problem. The bandit layer runs in **microseconds on one CPU core**; the full request (candidate scoring + compliance gate) is **milliseconds**.

### 3.1 Compute per approach

| Approach | Training compute | Serving compute | Honest note |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Linear contextual bandit** (LinUCB / LinTS) on α/β/γ | **None.** `A` at d=128 is ~64KB; Sherman-Morrison O(d²) updates; folds into nightly retrain or updates incrementally | **None.** Cached-inverse matvec, microseconds on **one CPU core** — GPU kernel-launch overhead alone would be *slower* | **$0 incremental hardware.** Runs on cores you already pay for. |
| **Neural-Linear bandit** (Bayesian last layer / small MLP reward head on frozen two-tower) | **One smallest-class GPU** (T4/L4/A10G, 16–24GB) for nightly train — minutes at 50K; CPU also works | **None.** Export ONNX, sub-ms on CPU | GPU is a *convenience*, not a requirement. Full NeuralUCB/Neural-TS need a p×p gradient-covariance inverse over all params → infeasible; that's why you restrict to the **last layer**. |
| **Conservative offline RL** (CQL/BCQ/IQL) | One A10G / L4 / A100-40GB; hours not days; tiny heads on 128-d embeddings | None if distilled to ONNX | **Hardware is a non-issue; the methodology is the problem.** Skip this tier. |
| **Existing two-tower retraining** | The GPU that's already in the plan | ONNX-on-CPU (already in plan) | Unchanged. |

### 3.2 The non-GPU infra that is 80%+ of the build

Off-policy learning and evaluation are **mathematically impossible** without these. Every item is non-GPU.

| # | Net-new item | What it is | Why it's the real cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Propensity / exposure logging + injected exploration** | Log, per event: candidate set, action taken, `π_log(a|x)`, gate decision. AND add controlled randomization (today's ranker is deterministic) | The #1 prerequisite. No propensities → no IPS/DR. Cannot be retrofitted onto history. A trader-trust + compliance event, not a backend tweak. |
| 2 | **Reward-attribution pipeline** | Async join of exposures → trades/feedback; defined attribution window; explicit negative-reward definition | Bond trades **lag** (execute hours-days later, settle T+1/T+2; often closed off-platform via voice/RFQ); a *non-trade* is ambiguous. The hardest, quietest risk. |
| 3 | **Feature-store train/serve consistency** | Point-in-time-correct context (same regime features, same recency-decayed s3, no future-feedback leakage) | Train/serve skew silently destroys bandits and is invisible in offline metrics. |
| 4 | **OPE harness** | IPS / SNIPS / DR estimators + reward model, as the offline safety gate | Lets you reject bad policies offline; produces the validation evidence for model risk. CPU batch job. |
| 5 | **Shadow → A/B → online-KPI infra** | Shadow-mode toggle, controlled A/B, KPI dashboards (trade conversion, revenue/rec, accept/dismiss/ignore, diversity/Gini, novelty/% new bonds) | Closes the online-eval gap the review flagged. |
| 6 | **Drift detector + retrain trigger** | PSI on features, reward-rate monitors, posterior decay/forgetting | 30%/quarter turnover + Fed/credit cycles mean a stale posterior degrades silently. |
| 7 | **Model-risk / SR 11-7 governance** | Documented purpose, independent validation, change-control over a self-updating policy, audit trail | In a regulated bank this is frequently the **actual critical path**. |

### 3.3 What Wells likely has vs. net-new

- **Stated for this system:** Kafka, ONNX serving, model registry + promotion gate, nightly retraining.
- **Likely at org level but unverified for Helios:** a point-in-time feature store, batch-GPU capacity, audit/lineage tooling. Don't assume desk-level availability.
- **Net-new (none need a GPU):** propensity logging + injected exploration, reward attribution, OPE harness, shadow/A-B, drift detector, SR 11-7 governance of a learning policy.

### Minimal vs. comfortable

- **Minimal (ship Phase-2 LinUCB safely):** existing CPU serving box, **no GPU**; net-new = propensity-logging schema + Kafka events, reward-attribution batch join, IPS/SNIPS OPE script, shadow toggle, exploration constrained by the existing gate. Reuses Kafka / feature store / registry / ONNX / nightly retrain.
- **Comfortable (room for neural bandit + rigorous OPE):** add **one shared T4/L4** (batch, nightly, <10% utilization), serving stays CPU/ONNX; add DR-OPE harness, automated A/B + KPI dashboards, drift detector, candidate-set logging.

> **The real spend is engineer-months on the pipeline, not silicon.** Compute is the non-issue; safe online learning under regulation is the work.

