# UPDATE — 2026-06-30 — XGBoost Reframe: What a Working Tree Baseline Does to the RL Roadmap

> Added after the user clarified: the team now has a **promising XGBoost baseline** (feature selection + labeling + scoring already done); the **neural two-tower is still early**. The prior conclusion (RL = contextual bandit at the re-rank layer, off-policy first, no GPU, augment-not-replace) **still holds** — but the XGBoost baseline *sharpens* the bandit's charter rather than shrinking it. Verified via a 3-angle adversarial workflow (modeling reframe · OPE/reward reuse · roadmap+compliance).

## TL;DR

- **XGBoost is the right-sized call.** At ~50K heterogeneous tabular rows, gradient-boosted trees beating neural nets is the *expected*, literature-backed result (Grinsztajn 2022; Shwartz-Ziv & Armon 2021). Ship it as the production ranker now; do **not** gate it on the two-tower. "Neural still early" is a non-issue for the *ranking* layer.
- **It SUBSUMES the linear weight-learner.** XGBoost learns a non-linear, context-conditioned `f(s1, s2, s3, context)` that strictly dominates three learned scalars. Formally **retire** "Phase-2 = learn α/β/γ" as an objective. The bandit's job shrinks to what supervised learning structurally *cannot* do: **exploration beyond the analyst-pitched set, epistemic uncertainty for directed explore/exploit, and off-policy correction.**
- **You're closer to OPE than you think.** The trained XGBoost relevance/conversion model *is* the q̂(x,a) regression component of the Direct Method — and the Direct-Method term inside Doubly-Robust. The "build a reward model" line item is largely done; what remains is plumbing (propensity + candidate-set logging) and a **reward-definition retarget to best-execution**, not a model build.
- **Compliance win.** An interpretable deterministic tree ranker + **monotonic constraints** (best-ex as a *provable invariant*) + SHAP per-decision attributions is the most defensible stack you can put in front of model-risk for a regulated best-ex surface — far more so than a neural policy.

## Where each model lives now

| Layer | Owner | Job | Justification |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Retrieval / embedding** | Two-tower (InfoNCE) | Embeddings + cold-start + large-space retrieval (client ~50K, all-time catalog ~100K); manufactures `s1` as an XGBoost feature; widens the candidate set | Cold-start (30%/qtr turnover), representation learning, bidirectional retrieval — jobs trees **don't** do. *Not* "sub-linear ANN over the active pool": at 500–1000 active bonds XGBoost can brute-force-score the whole pool, so retrieval isn't the bottleneck — the tower's real value is `s1` + cold-start embeddings. |
| **Ranking** | XGBoost | Learns the non-linear, context-conditioned combination of `s1`, `s2` (BWIC), `s3` (decayed feedback) + client/bond/regime context | Production scorer; replaces the linear blend; expected to win at this scale and stay the ranker for a long time. |
| **Exploration + off-policy** | Contextual bandit | Thin policy wrapper on top of XGBoost scores: epistemic uncertainty → directed exploration, propensity logging, IPS/SNIPS/DR OPE, compliance mask | The residual supervised learning *cannot* supply. **Wrap-not-replace.** |

### Does XGBoost subsume α·s1 + β·s2 + γ·s3? — split the claim by layer

- **YES, as the scorer.** XGBoost learns a non-linear, interaction-aware, context-conditioned combination of the same signals. The linear weighted sum dies. Retire "the bandit learns α/β/γ" as a literal objective.
- **NO, it does not obsolete the bandit.** The bandit's real role was never weight-learning — it was the **off-policy decision + de-biasing + counterfactual-evaluation** layer. Trained on analyst-pitched-only logs with no propensities, XGBoost *reproduces and amplifies* selection bias (closed-loop feedback collapse), optimizes whatever label it's given (reward-hacking if the label is "trade/revenue"), and does **zero** exploration.

> **XGBoost = scoring/value model. Contextual bandit = thin policy wrapper (propensity logging, OPE, compliance mask, bounded exploration).** XGBoost additionally serves as the DR reward model — it *complements*, it doesn't replace.

**Integration shape:** `policy = argmax over masked candidates of [ XGBoost_score + β · uncertainty_bonus ]`. XGBoost is the value backbone; the bandit is a thin, OPE-evaluable decision layer. The uncertainty term is the **last** increment, not a co-equal first step.

## Trees vs neural at 50K tabular — why XGBoost likely wins now

The two-tower being early is **not** a problem, because it was never supposed to win the *ranking* fight. Its job (recall, embeddings, asymmetric update cadence, cold-start warm-start) is one trees don't do. **Do not deprioritize the retriever on the strength of a tabular-ranking benchmark** — and do not re-litigate ranking with neural at this scale; that burns runway for no expected lift and risks regressing the working baseline.

## The big reuse — XGBoost as the OPE reward model

**The model you already built is the regression component of OPE.**

- **Direct Method (ship now, no propensities):** `V_DM(π) = (1/n) Σ_i Σ_{a∈C_i} π(a|x_i)·q̂(x_i,a)`. `C_i` = logged candidate set per impression. Use today to A/B re-rank variants offline. **Caveat:** DM is **biased** on the analyst-pitched distribution → treat as **directional/relative** (valid for ranking XGBoost variants when bias is common-mode); absolute lift stays untrustworthy until DR.
- **Doubly-Robust (after propensity logging):** `V_DR(π) = V_DM(π) + (1/n) Σ_i [π(a_i|x_i)/p_log(a_i|x_i)]·(r_i − q̂(x_i,a_i))`. Use **SNIPS / weight clipping** (clip ~10–20); report **effective sample size** `ESS=(Σw)²/Σw²` on every number — if ESS collapses the policy is off-support → route to shadow, don't trust the number.
- **Two hard caveats a model-risk review WILL catch:** (1) **q̂ must be retargeted to the best-execution reward `r`, not conversion/trade**, and calibrated on r's scale (isotonic/Platt) — a conversion-only q̂ launders the inventory-offload reward-hack through a "rigorous" OPE harness. (2) **Cross-fit q̂ out-of-fold** — DR's double-robustness fails when q̂ and propensities come from the *same* biased logs.
- **IPS-weighted training:** `sample_weight = π_target/p_log` (reduces to `1/p_log` only when target is uniform; a re-ranker usually wants the serving candidate-set distribution). Hard precondition: a reliable `p_log` — Helios never logged it, so fit a propensity model on pitching history; if pitching is driven by *unlogged* signals (verbal axes, relationship reads) unconfoundedness fails and IPS is biased regardless of clipping.
- **Uncertainty for exploration (trees give none):** separate **probability calibration** (cheap post-hoc — isotonic/Platt) from **epistemic uncertainty** (the structural gap). Pragmatic pick = a conjugate **Bayesian-linear / linear-Thompson (or LinUCB) head on a *frozen* XGBoost extractor**. On `[s1,s2,s3]` the posterior mean *literally is* α/β/γ **with credible intervals** — the Phase-2 weight-learner reborn *with* uncertainty, CPU microseconds, model-agnostic, auditable. (NGBoost/quantile = wrong quantity for UCB/TS; conformal = complementary abstention gate, not a sampling distribution.)

## The "promising results" trap — verify before you trust

Promising offline NDCG/AUC on analyst-pitched logs is **evidence the model agrees with the logging policy — not evidence of online best-execution value**, and it is *silent* on never-pitched bonds (exactly the region any re-ranker shifts mass toward). This is a **logging-policy bias the XGBoost baseline already shares**, not a bandit-only risk. Four checks before trusting the baseline:

| Check | What | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Temporal validation** | Train-past / test-future, per-quarter — **not** random k-fold | 30%/qtr turnover + drift; random folds leak future-bond signal and overstate the baseline |
| **Slice metrics** | AUC/NDCG/calibration on (a) cold/new bonds, (b) sparse-history clients, (c) **never-pitched** bonds | Headline metric is dominated by the well-covered pitched slice; the bandit operates on the thin slices |
| **Calibration** | Reliability curve + ECE/Brier; isotonic/Platt | A q̂ used as a *value* (not just rank) must be calibrated or DM/DR arithmetic is silently biased |
| **Propensity-weighted offline metric** | Recompute with IPS weights (weighted-AUC / SNIPS reward) | High headline NDCG but low propensity-weighted metric ⇒ model wins only on over-represented logged actions — the canonical selection-bias tell |

Plus a **reward-definition audit**: confirm the label encodes best-execution (price improvement / BWIC-consistent fill), not raw trade/revenue, *before* it becomes q̂.

**Disambiguate the baseline first — ask, don't predict.** "Different scoring approaches" + "labeling" most likely signals target/label definition + objective experimentation, which could be pointwise OR the pointwise→pairwise→listwise ladder. **Pointwise (`binary:logistic`) is at least as likely as listwise here** — the IPS/SNIPS/DR harness wants a calibrated `P(trade|·)`, not a relative listwise score; LambdaMART becomes natural only once graded relevance + query groups exist (usually later). Three questions: (1) what **objective** (`binary:logistic` vs `rank:pairwise` vs `rank:ndcg`, is `set_group` used)? (2) labels binary or graded, what defines a positive? (3) metric AUC/logloss (pointwise tell) vs NDCG@k/MAP@k (ranking tell) — and **is the output calibrated**?

## Revised sequenced roadmap

**Propensity/candidate logging is the true critical path** and is independent of which model produces the score.

| Phase | Work | Gate to advance |
|---|---|---|
| **0 — NOW (parallel)** | **A:** XGBoost hardening — **monotonic constraints** on best-ex features (price improvement, liquidity/BWIC quality, axe alignment), wire **SHAP** into the blotter. **B:** Propensity + candidate-set logging (define a softened logging policy even for a deterministic ranker). | XGBoost passes **temporal** offline eval AND logging is live capturing candidate sets + propensities |
| **1 — DR-OPE harness** | IPS → SNIPS → DR, reusing XGBoost as the DM/value component. Retarget reward to best-execution. Cross-fit q̂. | OPE validated: logged-A/B replay sanity, healthy ESS, CIs, support-coverage reported |
| **2 — Shadow mode** | XGBoost scores live, logs what it *would* recommend vs incumbent. Measure online best-ex KPIs. | Shadow non-inferior/better on online best-ex KPIs; model-risk sign-off on SHAP + monotonic-constraint doc |
| **3 — XGBoost live** | Deterministic ranker behind the **hard compliance gate**. **No exploration yet.** | — |
| **4 — Bandit (additive, later)** | Thin contextual bandit on live XGBoost: a **bounded stochastic exploration policy** (Thompson/ε over gate-approved top-K). Off-policy first via the Phase-1 harness, then bounded rate-limited exploration. This stochastic layer also supplies the non-degenerate propensities OPE needs for overlap. | OPE shows the bandit beats deterministic XGBoost **with CI**; model-risk approves exploration bounds + logging |

**Dataflow (compliance-critical):** `candidates → gate hard-mask → XGBoost score → bandit bounded perturbation (logged, rate-limited) → gate RE-ASSERTED → blotter`. The gate **brackets** exploration (mask first so the bandit explores only legal arms, re-assert last). The served action, the logged (action, propensity, candidate_set), and reward attribution must all reflect the **post-mask** distribution, or OPE is biased.

**Neural two-tower** stays on its **own track** as a retrieval/candidate-generation upgrade judged by **recall@k**; it feeds better candidates into the *same* XGBoost ranker. It does **not** gate the bandit. Promote only when it beats the current retriever on recall@k.

**What not to do:** (1) don't rush the two-tower to "look advanced"; (2) don't let offline metrics greenlight live deployment; (3) don't bolt a bandit on before propensity logging; (4) don't put exploration downstream of the compliance gate.

## Compliance angle — the defensible pattern

**Interpretable deterministic tree ranker + thin logged/rate-limited exploration + hard compliance gate.**

| Control | Mechanism | Maps to |
|---|---|---|
| Conceptual soundness | SHAP per-decision + global importance answer "why was this bond ranked here" | SR 11-7 |
| **Best-ex as model invariant** | **Monotonic constraints** — monotone in price improvement / liquidity quality ⇒ "a strictly better price never ranks lower, all else equal" is *provable, auditable, survives retraining* | The single strongest sentence in the model-risk doc |
| Bounded exploration | Thin, logged, rate-limited, reversible; propensities logged for retrospective OPE | Auditability |
| Hard compliance gate last | Suitability / restricted-list / best-ex can **never** be violated by the learner | FINRA 5310 / MiFID II |

**Two load-bearing qualifications:** (1) the durable artifact is the **monotone constraint (a guarantee)**; SHAP is *supporting evidence* that can drift under feature correlation — get each constraint's **direction** right with a compliance partner (a wrong-signed constraint bakes a violation in as an "invariant"). (2) Monotonicity/SHAP defend the **scorer, not the bandit** — the invariant only transfers if the bandit's exploration is **sign-constrained**. Doc sentence: *"The scoring function is monotone in price-improvement and liquidity/BWIC quality by construction, AND the exploration policy is sign-constrained to preserve that monotonicity."* Also: ε-greedy is the **wrong** exploration shortcut — uniform exploration is harder to defend under best-ex than **uncertainty-directed** exploration.

## Interview update (spoken, 2–3 sentences)

> "We deliberately started with the right-sized model — a gradient-boosted tree ranker — because it's both strong on tabular fixed-income signals and far more defensible to model risk: SHAP gives per-recommendation explanations and monotonic constraints let us encode best-execution as a provable invariant, like 'a strictly better price never ranks lower.' The XGBoost model also doubles as the reward/value model for doubly-robust off-policy evaluation, so we're closer to a real OPE harness than a model-build away — the gating work is propensity logging and retargeting the reward to best-execution, not training. The RL piece is a deliberate *later* augmentation: a thin contextual bandit that adds uncertainty-directed exploration on top of the tree ranker, gated behind OPE because online exploration is unshippable under FINRA 5310 — and the neural retriever is a separate, parallel candidate-generation track that feeds the same ranker and doesn't block the bandit."

